   "text": {
    "L1": "Barter means exchanging goods or services directly, without money.",
    "L2": "It works only when each person wants what the other person has. It also lacks a common measure of value.",
    "L3": "A goat cannot be divided into eggs, and some goods can spoil. These problems make barter difficult.",
    "L4": "For example, a potter wants a sari, but the sari-seller wants wheat, not pots.",
    "L5": "The potter could trade pots for wheat first, then trade the wheat for the sari. Money makes this simpler.",
    "L6": "Exchange developed from commodity money to metal coins, paper notes, bank money and UPI payments.",
    "L7": "Money is a medium of exchange, a measure of value and a store of value.",
    "L8": "A note has value because people trust it and the law makes it legal tender, not because of its paper.",
    "L9": "UPI moves money from one bank account to another. Never share your PIN or OTP, and check the receiver."
   },
